/*!
    \page tutorial-ai-and-wildlife.html
    \title Tutorial 10: AI Opponents and Wildlife
    \previouspage {Tutorial 9: Economy, Bases and the Radial Menu}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 11: Ending the Match}

    The computer races use the same economy, actions and orders as the player. Only the
    decisions come from code.

    \section1 Facts

    Before each decision the core collects what the AI needs:

    \quotefromfile backend/game.cpp
    \skipto ai_context_t context{
    \printuntil };

    \section1 Decisions

    \l ai_planner_t is a pure function. The rules in short:

    \list 1
        \li Enemies near the base: defend, spawn units.
        \li Army too small: spawn more.
        \li Low on resources and a wild creature close by: go hunting.
        \li Army big enough: attack an enemy base.
        \li Spare resources: training, upgrades, research.
    \endlist

    The first rule in code:

    \quotefromfile backend/ai_planner.cpp
    \skipto const auto under_attack
    \printuntil return decision;

    \section1 Difficulty

    Easy, normal and hard use the same planner with other numbers from
    \c data/ai_profiles.json:

    \quotefromfile data/ai_profiles.json
    \skipto "hard": {
    \printuntil }

    \section1 Wildlife

    \l wildlife_spawner_t plans deer, wolves and troll lairs on the scheduler, each time
    near another race:

    \quotefromfile backend/wildlife_spawner.cpp
    \skipto void wildlife_spawner_t::start(
    \printuntil }

    A killed wild creature pays a reward. It is split between the races by the damage
    they dealt. Hunting pays, but it leaves your base open.

*/
